text:
maria: Heads-up — ran into a weird runtime gap running the js SDK in Cloudflare Workers. Some deps assume Node globals (process, Buffer) and types blow up in TS. Anyone solved this without forking the lib? :thinking:

tom: We hit this too. short answer: add tiny runtime shims and restrict your bundle target. Workers = no Node APIs, only Web globals.

sasha: +1. Our approach: 1) avoid node-only libs, 2) provide small shims for process.env and Buffer where absolutely needed, 3) tweak TS to include 'dom' lib.

maria: Example? my build fails on `import EventEmitter from 'events'` and `process.env`.

dan: For EventEmitter use a tiny Emitter polyfill or rewrite to use simple callbacks. For process.env, inject at build time via define plugin (esbuild/rollup). Example esbuild flag: `--define:process.env.NODE_ENV="production"`.

luis: If you must shim Buffer for a dep, add this early in your entry file: ```
if (typeof globalThis.Buffer === 'undefined') {
  // minimal Buffer shim for libs that call Buffer.from
  globalThis.Buffer = { from: (s) => new TextEncoder().encode(s) }
}
```

maria: Nice. What about typings? TS still complains about `process` in code that is compiled away by define.

tom: Add a local ambient type in your repo: ```
// types/global.d.ts
declare namespace NodeJS {
  interface ProcessEnv { NODE_ENV?: string }
}
declare var process: { env: NodeJS.ProcessEnv } | undefined;
```
Then include it in tsconfig `typeRoots` or `include` so the compiler knows.

sasha: Also set `lib: ["es2021","dom"]` in tsconfig. Workers runtime exposes some Web APIs but not Node ones — telling TS about dom/ws/es is critical.

dan: If you control the SDK build, consider publishing two entries: `module` (ESM for edge) and `main` (CJS for node). That avoids conditional shims for many users.

maria: SDK is external lib, we don't control build. For now I want a local wrapper around the SDK to adapt the surface to Workers. Any patterns?

luis: Wrapper pattern: create `edge-adapter.ts` which imports the SDK, replaces things at runtime (if present) and exports a trimmed API. Example: ```
import * as SDK from 'the-sdk';
if (typeof globalThis.process === 'undefined') {
  (globalThis as any).process = { env: {} }
}
export const client = new SDK.Client({ fetchFn: fetch }); // pass native fetch explicitly if SDK supports it
```
This keeps runtime changes localized.

maria: Passing fetch explicitly is key — we had a bug where the SDK tried to require('node-fetch') fallback. If you can pass the fetch impl it skips node-only codepaths.

tom: Another trick: use conditional exports at bundle time to map `optionalDependencies` that use Node-only APIs to noop versions when building for workers. esbuild `inject` option can alias modules.

sasha: For CI, add a `workers` test job that runs a tiny miniflare suite to catch regressions early. Saved us from shipping a broken bundle.

maria: Good call. Any gotchas with AbortSignal or streaming in Workers?

dan: AbortSignal exists as `AbortController` in Workers, but some Node libs feature slightly different behavior. If SDK exposes streaming via node streams, that's a problem — prefer SDK APIs that use Web Streams or async iterators.

luis: Also watch for globalThis.crypto differences. Workers provide webcrypto, but some libs expect Node's `crypto.randomBytes`. You can shim with `globalThis.crypto.getRandomValues` wrapper.

maria: Thanks all — plan: add small shims, create edge-adapter wrapper, add types/global.d.ts, and CI job with miniflare. I'll send a PR with the adapter and CI config.

